\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 It's week 1!  This is uncharted territory for me, so bear with me as I sort out this cyberworld.   I taught this topic at Power Paws Camp this past year and I do love it for both the very forward dogs and the more thoughtful dogs.  I have one of each, Rich would like to do things with the least amount of collection required and Vic looks for opportunties to show off his collection skills.   I believe dogs, given the opportunity, will figure out what is fastest for them.  So, I do not worry about how "tight" each turn is, I look for the correct response to what I cued. If Rich does something in one stride, where Vic will do it in two, but Rich knows where he is going next, I choose not to fight for a "prettier" turn.  I mention this, because if I really want to see a collection stride, I have to have distances that will make it required for my dog to offer it.  Some of the distances I use here, aren't distances that Rich requires a collection stride to give me the jumping effort needed to adjust for the turn.   Yes, the turns are wider, but my stopwatch says they are faster than when I insist on true collection. \par
Like I said, this topic came to me last year after camp, when I became obsessed with the belief that our dogs first clue that a turn is happening, is a change in our speed.  Personally, I am a very meat and potatoes handler, I rarely practice "fancy" handling, I find that my meat and potatoes lets me do the fancy handling, but the fancy handling does not help my meat and potatoes.  There are pros and cons of all handling choices and I have students that have successfully made very different choices than me, so I truly don't care what choices you make for handling the sequences.  I do care that the dogs are rewarded for responses to cues.  And I know that by rewarding the primary cue of decel, your dog will respond better to your secondary turning cues.  :)  Decel will always happen before I turn, so if my dog isn't noticing the first part of my turning cue, I am in trouble.  \par
We are too quick to say "what should I have done differently?" instead of questioning if our dog read our cue correctly.  If I am standing still near a jump, I expect my dog to take the jump and come back to me. They should come directly back to my front, I should not have to turn for them to return or have to move for them to return and I do not want to see any drifting across the front of me before they return.  After that it would come down to my timing, which is it's own challenge.  ;)  If I am moving forward while after they have committed to a jump, they are correct to think I want them to keep going forward.  If I have changed my speed before they have taken an obstacle, I want them to know that means they are turning.  When would it mean anything else?  \par
A rarely talked about perk of a dog that reads decel is we can be late with our secondary cue.  Let me use this moment to tell a story I often tell my students about dogs that have great at understanding decel.  There is a team I see every year when I go back east and I always tell the handler she's "late".  She came out to Power Paws Camp a couple years ago and people were confused why I was telling her she was late when the turns were so good.  The turns were great, because her dog read decel cues so well, she was late because she could have been driving the next line sooner.  What they have taught me is, "I can afford to be late, if my dog reads decel".  Obviously I do not want to be late in driving the next line, but I'll take any advantage I can get, especially if it covers me not being a perfect handler. ;)    And I don't think it matters if you are doing a counter rotation (reverse spin, jaakko, ketschker) or a post turn, if my dog knew on take off where I expected them to drive to next, it's my timing what tells them what to do with it afterwards.\par
My rewards for pure decel are located at my front for a straight return.  If I start turning for a front cross or a shoulder turn, then I am rewarding a secondary cue (what to do with the decel cue), instead of rewarding the decel.  I LOVE breaking things down to the most basic behaviors, probably a little too much, but I am an exception to that and I have found over the years, most of what I can overdo, my students should be doing a lot more of.   ;)  Initially I am going to reward basically anything in the ballpark of "come back!" that I can get.  It is not fair to expect a perfect response, if my dog isn't sure what I'm asking for.  As my confidence in my dog's understanding increases I can hold out for better responses.  \par
Our first exerises of this week, start with rewarding a correct response to decel.  No momentum, just jump and come back.  I did my video demos this week, in less than 15 minutes, after weeks off.  The initial chunk of time off was planned, but there's been this wet stuff coming from the sky, that I haven't seen in year....  So, Rich is basically feral and Vic, the dog that makes me look taller/smarter/prettier/thinner has a sore wrist from romping in the mud.   Where I am using a toy, an enthusiastically given food reward would work.  \par
First exercise is no momentum, just rewarding your dog for coming straight back.  You can see on the video that I use my outside hand to push Rich to a better position while rewarding the first try.  I liked where he landed, but did not like how he fishtailed after the reward, some of that is going to happen, but by using my hand to push him to a better position, I can get more info across in that effort.  Next one is much better, still not perfect.  Once I think my dog is showing good understanding with a single jump (and at any point in confusion, lowering the jumps is always a good option), then I will add the momentum of the tunnel.  If you do not have a tunnel, send them around a wing or a cone and you can always set up half of the excerice if you do not have room for the full set up.   The last exercise of these is to stop moving between the two jumps in an ambiguous location to indicate the answer is just come to me, don't take a jump.  If I am within 5' of a jump, it should be taken unless I tell them not to take it and personally I don't practice that.  This exercise is to remind the dogs when we want handler focus, not always can we be showing them the next obstacle, many times I can just show them the next line.\par
After you have done those, I would like you to try the numbered sequences.  I only demo one direction, because it's been raining and my time was super limited.  I want you to first reward every single decel cue given. 1, 2, 3 reward, 3, 4 reward, 4, 5 reward etc...  After you have done that and are happy with your teammate's responses, I want you to handle it as you normally would, just be sure to recognize you "slow down THEN..".   THEN and AND are very different, be sure the decel cue happens before the secondary cues.  :) \par
When running the complete exercises, I ran them a little different than I would, if it were happening on a "real" course.  On a "real" course, I would use a threadle arm for the wrap turns (because of the 180 jump options).  I do think it is important to run as you would, but with these little skill drills, I like practicing with as few turning cues as possible.  And really, isn't that our goal?  How to get around a course as smoothly as possible using the fewest turning cues as possible?  And I have found it is easier to add more, than it is to edit.  :) \par
Some trouble shooting...  If your dog scans or takes another obstacle after you have shown decel, show them what you would do, if you wanted that obstacle.  Most of the time, if we show the dog the cues for what they are offering and then go back to what we are asking,  we will get a different response.  It gets you two for one.  You reward  what the dog thought the answer was and what you want the answer to be.  :)  You should only mark a knocked bar, if you know your dog understands what you were asking them to do.  Which goes back to, lower the jumps if you know they could be confused.  At the end of the day, these are running exercises, since our dogs can not see decel without first seeing excel.  I love Jim Basic's saying "it's not how fast you run, it's how WHEN you run".  Each of us is going to be able to run with different ability, but when we move to the next thing won't be that different.  I would love to be on top of each turn, but I can't always get there, if I stop moving before my dog has taken an obstacle, that means "come back" whether I am on top of it or behind it.  Timing of decel matters more than location to decel, so I will do these exercises changing where I am in relation to the turns to make sure my teammate is noticing when I am not going forward.  It shouldn't matter if I am on top of the turn jump or I have fallen behind (forward send).  Make sure your teammate isn't expecting you to be in perfect postion all the time.\par
And here is where they cyberworld confusion starts happening for me, I'm hoping this is enough for you to go on, if it is confusing let me know.  \par
Oh!!!  You get to use postion, motion/lack of motion and some eye contact \ul ONLY\ulnone , no talking/rotating/arms etc.  Strip it down to the most basic cues and make sure the dogs are responding to those.  :)   You can add whatever you want when running full sequences, but personally I prefer to keep skill drills stripped down.  And if you feel naked, let me assure you, the most advanced group at camp last year told me that my session made them feel like I had made them run without any clothes on.  ;)  \par
